{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"PRIORITY RAISED TO 0. New evidence: this defect is currently failing ~46% of ALL CI runs repo-wide, not just the changes that first surfaced it. Over the last 60 Gerrit Verified runs (2026-09-03 14:27Z to 09-04 03:01Z): 27 success, 23 failure of 50 completed. It blocks every open change in the repository.\n\nIT IS NOT INTERMITTENT, AND THAT CORRECTS HOW I FRAMED IT. I posted a recheck on change 2594 describing this as an 'intermittent race'. That was wrong. It is a FIXED, UNBOUNDED-COST fixture measured against a FIXED budget, and the cost has grown until it straddles the 300s cliff. Measured setup times for test_emitted_bpmn_has_no_illegal_ids on jobs that PASSED, from --durations output:\n  40.96s  (run 33815025971, py3.11)\n  101.86s (run 33829699178, py3.12)\n  201.58s (run 33829352524, py3.12)\n  271.35s (run 33819649550, py3.12)  <- 90% of the 300s budget, barely passed\nAnything over 300s trips timeout_method='thread', which calls os._exit(1) and kills the worker. So which matrix cells fail varies run to run while the underlying cost does not -- that is why a recheck did not clear it and why it lands on different Python versions each time. Corroboration that it is a timeout and not OOM or a segfault: no [rebar-ci-timing] warning for bpmn_harness appears in ANY failing log, because the fixture never completed setup.\n\nONSET IS SHARP: last fully clean run 33811108275 at 2026-09-03T22:02:43Z; first gw0 crash run 33813339060 at 22:30:05Z; zero occurrences from 09-03 01:04Z through 22:02Z. No commit merged in that window touches the e2e/Node/CI path.\n\nTHREE THINGS THE FIX MUST ACCOUNT FOR:\n\n1. PLAYWRIGHT DOMINATES THE COLD INSTALL and is a devDependency needed only by browser_runner, not by bpmn_harness. Removing it from the bpmn_harness provisioning path is the single cheapest lever and may alone drop the cost below the cliff. Evaluate this even if the structural fix lands too.\n\n2. THE VERIFIED LANE RUNS WORKFLOWS FROM TRUSTED MAIN, so a CI-side pre-provisioning step CANNOT be validated from a patchset -- it only takes effect once merged. Plan for that: do not expect a green Verified on the patchset that introduces the CI step, and do not read its absence as failure.\n\n3. THE PORTABLE HALF IS STILL REQUIRED. A CI-only pre-provision step does not satisfy project.portability. The in-process path must also work with no CI provider -- either provisioning outside any test's budget (a pytest_sessionstart or collection hook) or an explicit marked timeout naming the provisioning cost.\n\nmacOS never exercises this: DEFAULT_SUITE_MARKS restricts the macOS job to platform_compat, and test_editor_e2e appears zero times in the macOS log. So the matrix hides the defect on one of four cells."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788491301698367001,"uuid":"9ef6a643-edb3-4611-b424-c0a1cbecb87d"}